BT 14 TL 56 780 Td
/F2 15 Tf (The five SOLO levels: overview) Tj T*
T*
/F1 11 Tf (SOLO describes five levels of increasing structural complexity.) Tj T*
T*
/F1 11 Tf (Level 0, prestructural. The answer misses the point: it is irrelevant, repeats the) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (question or contains only isolated words.) Tj T*
T*
/F1 11 Tf (Level 1, unistructural. One relevant aspect is given correctly, without connection to) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (anything else.) Tj T*
T*
/F1 11 Tf (Level 2, multistructural. Several relevant aspects are given, but they are treated) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (independently, like items in a list.) Tj T*
T*
/F1 11 Tf (Level 3, relational. The aspects are integrated into a coherent whole. The student) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (explains how and why they are connected.) Tj T*
T*
/F1 11 Tf (Level 4, extended abstract. The integrated whole is taken to a higher level of) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (abstraction: generalised to a new domain, used to form a hypothesis, or critically) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (questioned.) Tj T*
T*
/F1 11 Tf (The movement from level 1 to level 2 is a quantitative change: the student knows more.) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (The movement from level 2 to level 3 is a qualitative change: the student understands) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (the relations. This qualitative step is what teachers usually mean by "real) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (understanding".) Tj T*
ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 290 40 Td (2) Tj ET
endstream
