/F2 15 Tf (Multistructural answers: knowing more) Tj T*
T*
/F1 11 Tf (A multistructural answer to the same question: "The Earth is a sphere. It rotates. The) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (Sun shines on one side. A day lasts 24 hours.") Tj T*
T*
/F1 11 Tf (Every statement is true and relevant, yet the answer is a list. The student does not say) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (how rotation produces the alternation of day and night at one place. If the teacher) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (changes the question slightly, for example "What would happen if the Earth rotated twice) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (as slowly?", the student cannot answer, because the facts are not connected into a) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (mechanism.) Tj T*
T*
/F1 11 Tf (Typical verbs are "describe", "list", "enumerate", "classify" and "combine".) Tj T*
T*
/F1 11 Tf (Multistructural answers are dangerous for assessment because they look impressive. They) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (are long, they contain correct terms and they are often delivered confidently. A grader) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (who counts points gives them a high mark. This is how a student can receive good grades) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (and still not understand: the assessment rewarded quantity.) Tj T*
T*
/F1 11 Tf (Seeing "the trees but not the forest" is the usual description of this level.) Tj T*
