T*
/F1 11 Tf (SOLO is useful at three points of course design.) Tj T*
T*
/F1 11 Tf (Writing outcomes. The verb of an intended learning outcome shows the SOLO level that is) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (expected. "List the stages" asks for a multistructural performance. "Explain how the) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (stages depend on each other" asks for a relational one. This connects SOLO with) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (constructive alignment.) Tj T*
T*
/F1 11 Tf (Asking questions. A base question often produces a multistructural answer. Follow-up) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (questions reveal the real level: "Why?", "Can you give your own example?", "What if this) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (condition changes?", "Here is a statement, where is the error?" A memorised answer) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (survives the base question but not the follow-ups.) Tj T*
T*
/F1 11 Tf (Writing rubrics. Instead of counting points, a rubric describes each level for the) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (particular question: what an answer with one aspect looks like, with several unrelated) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (aspects, with integrated aspects, with generalisation. The grade then follows the) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (highest level that the student sustained with evidence.) Tj T*
T*
/F1 11 Tf (Good graders also separate structure from style. Length, fluency and a confident tone) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (are not evidence of a higher level.) Tj T*
