/F2 15 Tf (A model of effective feedback) Tj T*
T*
/F1 11 Tf (Hattie and Timperley \(2007\) describe feedback as an answer to three questions asked by) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (the learner.) Tj T*
T*
/F1 11 Tf (Where am I going? This is feed up: the goal and the criteria of success.) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (How am I going? This is feed back: evidence about present performance in relation to the) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (goal.) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (Where to next? This is feed forward: what to do in order to improve.) Tj T*
T*
/F1 11 Tf (Feedback can be directed at four levels. Task level: whether the answer is correct.) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (Process level: the strategy used to produce the answer. Self-regulation level: how the) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (student monitors and directs his or her own learning. Self level: personal evaluation) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (such as "you are a clever student".) Tj T*
T*
/F1 11 Tf (Feedback about process and self-regulation is the most powerful for deep learning.) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (Feedback at the self level, including praise, is the least effective, because it carries) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (no information about the task.) Tj T*
T*
/F1 11 Tf (Useful feedback is therefore specific, refers to criteria, points to evidence in the) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (student's own work and names the next step, for example which pages of the course) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (material to read again.) Tj T*
ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 290 40 Td (9) Tj ET
