/F2 15 Tf (Oral questioning, integrity and human judgement) Tj T*
T*
/F1 11 Tf (When students can obtain fluent text from a generative AI tool, written answers to) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (predictable questions no longer show who understands. Software that claims to detect) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (AI-generated text is not a solution: studies of such detectors report both false) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (accusations and missed cases, so their output cannot be treated as proof.) Tj T*
T*
/F1 11 Tf (A more robust response is to change the format of assessment. In an oral examination the) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (examiner builds each follow-up question on the words the student has just said. A) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (memorised or borrowed answer cannot be extended on demand, while real understanding can.) Tj T*
T*
/F1 11 Tf (Oral assessment has its own risks. It may cause anxiety, it favours fluent speakers, and) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (judgements can be affected by bias. These risks are reduced by a transparent rubric) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (shown in advance, by practice sessions without consequences, by recording the dialogue,) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (and by grading the structure of the answer rather than its style.) Tj T*
T*
